==English==

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|la|vulturinus||vulture-like}}.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Pertaining to or having characteristics of [[vulture]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1912|author=w:P. G. Wodehouse|title=w:The Prince and Betty|location=New York|publisher=W.J. Watt|chapter=2|page=10|url=https://archive.org/details/princebetty00wodeuoft
|passage=Somewhat below the middle height, he was lean of body and '''vulturine''' of face.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=w:Andrea Levy|title={{w|Small Island (novel)|Small Island}}|location=London|publisher=Review|chapter=6|pages=87–88|url=https://archive.org/details/smallisland00levy
|passage=Taking the cooked bird in her gnarled hands she stripped off the flesh with the few teeth she still had left in her head, gnawing on it with a '''vulturine''' concentration until it was just grey bones.}}
# [[predaceous|Predaceous]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1806|author={{w|Sir John Barrow, 1st Baronet|John Barrow}}|title=Travels into the Interior of Southern Africa|location=London|publisher=T. Cadell & W. Davies|volume=I|chapter=2|page=178|url=https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/011549826
|passage=A species of crow in vast numbers is generally found to attend the larger kind of birds of prey. It is uncommonly bold and ravenous, and all its habits are '''vulturine''' {{...}}}}

